# Start one external and one internal job in the background, then use bare `fg` twice
# to bring each one to the foreground in turn, confirming control genuinely moves
# between two distinct jobs -- each job prints its own distinct marker right before it
# finishes, so "did the right one actually run to completion" is a plain substring
# check, not a guess based on timing.
#
# The internal job is wrapped in a `{...} &` block rather than written as a bare
# `sleep ...; echo ... &` line: a trailing `&` only backgrounds the single Job it's
# attached to (see `Job`'s grammar rule in lalrparser.lalrpop), not a whole
# `;`-separated sequence of jobs -- `sleep ...; echo ... &` would run the `sleep`
# synchronously in the foreground (blocking this script for a second) and background
# only the near-instant `echo` after it, never producing a second genuinely-concurrent
# background job at all. Confirmed directly against the debug binary before writing
# this test. `{...} &` backgrounds the whole block as a single job instead.
#
# Bare `fg` (no job id) operates on the *most recently* backgrounded job -- a LIFO
# stack, see `GlobalState::take_last_background_job` -- confirmed directly against the
# debug binary. Since the internal job is started second here, the first `fg` resumes
# it, and the second `fg` resumes the external one.
$crush_bin := $(crush:env["CRUSH_BIN"])
$t := $(vt:create $crush_bin)
$t:wait_for "# "

$t:write "cmd sh -c 'sleep 1; echo EXTERNAL_DONE' &\n"
$t:wait_for "# "
$t:write "{sleep $(duration:of seconds=1); echo INTERNAL_DONE} &\n"
$t:wait_for "# "

$t:write "fg\n"
$t:wait_for "INTERNAL_DONE"
$t:wait_for "# "

$t:write "fg\n"
$t:wait_for "EXTERNAL_DONE"
$t:wait_for "# "

$t:close
echo "ok"
